{
  "slug": "en-y",
  "number": 16,
  "part": 4,
  "title": "En and y",
  "tagline": "Two tiny pronouns English barely has: places, quantities and everyday expressions.",
  "goal": "Use y for places and things introduced by à, use en for quantities and things introduced by de, place them in the sentence and understand expressions like on y va or j'en ai marre.",
  "minutes": 11,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "por-que",
      "title": "Why they're tricky",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "They're short, they go before the verb and English often leaves them out. But in French they're used **all the time**, and leaving them out sounds odd."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Tu vas au cinéma ? — Oui, j'y vais.",
              "tr": "Are you going to the cinema? — Yes, I'm going (there)."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Tu veux du gâteau ? — Oui, j'en veux.",
              "tr": "Do you want some cake? — Yes, I want some."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "y",
      "title": "Y: places and things with à",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Replaces",
            "Example",
            "With y"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "A **place** (*à, au, en, chez, dans, sur…*)",
              "*Je vais à Paris.*",
              "*J'{y} vais.*"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "*Il habite en France.*",
              "*Il {y} habite.*"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "*Les clés sont dans le tiroir.*",
              "*Les clés {y} sont.*"
            ],
            [
              "A **thing** introduced by **à**",
              "*Je pense à mon examen.*",
              "*J'{y} pense.*"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "*Tu réponds au message ?*",
              "*Tu {y} réponds ?*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "*y* is roughly «there» or «about it». You already know it from *il y a* (there is, there are)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "en",
      "title": "En: quantities and things with de",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Replaces",
            "Example",
            "With en"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "A **partitive** [[6]]",
              "*Tu veux du pain ?*",
              "*Oui, j'{en} veux.*"
            ],
            [
              "A **quantity** (the number stays)",
              "*J'ai trois chats.*",
              "*J'{en} ai trois.*"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "*Il boit beaucoup de café.*",
              "*Il {en} boit beaucoup.*"
            ],
            [
              "An **origin** with *de*",
              "*Je reviens de Paris.*",
              "*J'{en} reviens.*"
            ],
            [
              "A thing after a **verb + de**",
              "*Tu parles de ton voyage ?*",
              "*Oui, j'{en} parle.*"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "*J'ai besoin de mon téléphone.*",
              "*J'{en} ai besoin.*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "With *en*, the quantity goes **at the end**: *Tu as des frères ? — Oui, j'en ai deux.* (Yes, I have two.) You can't drop *en* the way English drops «of them»."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "prueba",
      "title": "Y or en?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "If there's…",
            "Use",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**à** (*au, aux*) or a place",
              "*y*",
              "*Je pense à mes vacances → J'y pense.*"
            ],
            [
              "**de** (*du, de la, des*) or a quantity",
              "*en*",
              "*Je parle de mes vacances → J'en parle.*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "Check the preposition of the **French** verb, not the English one: *penser à* (think about) → *y*; *parler de* (talk about) → *en*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "personas",
      "title": "With people, other pronouns",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*y* and *en* are for **things and places**. For **people** you use other pronouns [[15]]:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "With a thing",
            "With a person"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*Je pense à mon examen → J'y pense.*",
              "*Je pense à Marie → Je pense à elle.*"
            ],
            [
              "*Je parle de mon voyage → J'en parle.*",
              "*Je parle de Paul → Je parle de lui.*"
            ],
            [
              "*Je réponds à la lettre → J'y réponds.*",
              "*Je réponds à ma mère → Je lui réponds.*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "posicion",
      "title": "Where they go",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Situation",
            "With y",
            "With en"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Present",
              "*J'y vais.*",
              "*J'en mange.*"
            ],
            [
              "Negative",
              "*Je n'y vais pas.*",
              "*Je n'en mange pas.*"
            ],
            [
              "Near future",
              "*Je vais y aller.*",
              "*Je vais en manger.*"
            ],
            [
              "Passé composé",
              "*J'y suis allé.*",
              "*J'en ai mangé.*"
            ],
            [
              "Imperative",
              "*Vas-y !*",
              "*Manges-en !*"
            ],
            [
              "Together: always *y* before *en*",
              "*Il y en a.* (there is some)",
              "*Il n'y en a plus.* (there's none left)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "In the imperative, -er verbs get their s back before *y* and *en* to allow the liaison [[14]]: *Va !* → *Vas-y !* · *Mange !* → *Manges-en !*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "expresiones",
      "title": "Everyday expressions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Expression",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*On y va !*",
              "Let's go!"
            ],
            [
              "*Vas-y ! · Allez-y !*",
              "Go ahead!"
            ],
            [
              "*Ça y est !*",
              "Done! That's it!"
            ],
            [
              "*Il y a…*",
              "There is…, there are…"
            ],
            [
              "*Je m'en vais.*",
              "I'm off. I'm leaving."
            ],
            [
              "*J'en ai marre !*",
              "I'm fed up! (informal)"
            ],
            [
              "*Je n'y peux rien.*",
              "There's nothing I can do about it."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
